2.2 Assumptions in the Black-Scholes model
There are several idealized assumptions in the Black–Scholes model:

trading is possible continuously in time;
arbitrary fractions of the stock can be bought or sold;
short selling is allowed;
arbitrary amounts of money can be invested or borrowed at the same risk-free rate \(r\);
there are no transaction costs or taxes;
the stock pays no dividends;
the parameters \(\mu\), \(\sigma\), and \(r\) are constant.

These assumptions are clearly idealizations. Their purpose is to isolate the central mechanism behind the Black–Scholes model: continuous dynamic hedging.

3.1 The Black-Scholes formula







Theorem (The Black–Scholes formula).
Define \[
d_1(t,s)
=
\frac{
\ln(s/K)
+
\left(r+\frac12\sigma^2\right)(T-t)
}{
\sigma\sqrt{T-t}
},
\quad \text{ and }
d_2(t,s)
=
d_1(t,s)-\sigma\sqrt{T-t}.
\] Let \(N\) denote the cumulative distribution function of a standard normal random variable.
If there exist are no arbitrage opportunities in the Black-Scholes market, a european put and call option can be perfectly replicated by a hedging portfolio. The corresponding price and number of shares in the replicating portfolio are \[
\boxed{
P_t
=
Ke^{-r(T-t)}N\!\left(-d_2(t,S_t)\right)
-
S_tN\!\left(-d_1(t,S_t)\right).
}
\quad \text{ and }\quad
\boxed{
\Delta_t
=
N\!\left(d_1(t,S_t)\right)-1.
}
\] for a put option with strike \(K\) and maturity time \(T\) at time \(t&lt;T\). For the analogous call option, they are \[
\boxed{
C_t
=
S_tN\!\left(d_1(t,S_t)\right)
-
Ke^{-r(T-t)}N\!\left(d_2(t,S_t)\right),
}
\quad \text{ and }\quad
\boxed{
\Delta_t^{\mathrm{call}}
=
N\!\left(d_1(t,S_t)\right).
}
\]

After this chapter, you should be able to:

describe random variables, their distributions, and key probability concepts;
calculate and interpret expectations, variances, covariances, and moments;
distinguish different modes of convergence of random variables;
explain the law of large numbers (LLN) and central limit theorem (CLT) and their statistical implications;
calculate and interpret conditional expectations and their properties;
explain the optimality of conditional expectation for prediction and distinguish population prediction errors from empirical errors;
use Python simulations to explore distributions, convergence, and prediction.







TipHow to use this chapter



This is a refresher, not a first course in probability in which we provide a particularly computational view on the most essential concepts. We recall the central definitions and results, work through a few examples, and use simulations to connect mathematical statements to observable behavior. Try to predict the outcome of each experiment before running its code. The statistical interpretation is more important than the particular Python commands.


Reading: Wasserman (2004), All of Statistics, selected material from Chapters 1–5: probability, random variables, expectation, inequalities, and convergence. Book PDF.
